1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,804
Good morning.

2
00:00:00,804 --> 00:00:06,927
In the last few lectures I've been talking about the history of domestic building

3
00:00:06,927 --> 00:00:07,360
construction.

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:14,000
But today I want to begin looking at some contemporary, experimental designs for housing.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,174
So, I'm going to start with a house which is constructed more

6
00:00:19,174 --> 00:00:21,330
or less under the ground.

7
00:00:21,330 --> 00:00:28,103
And one of the interesting things about this project is that the owners-both professionals

8
00:00:28,103 --> 00:00:31,490
but not architects-wanted to be closely involved,

9
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:35,360
So they decided to manage the project themselves.

10
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:43,500
Their chief aim was to create somewhere that was as environmentally-friendly as possible.

11
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:51,665
But at the same time they wanted to live somewhere peaceful-they'd both grown up in a rural area

12
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:53,480
and disliked urban life.

13
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:58,426
So the first thing they did was to look for a site.

14
00:00:58,426 --> 00:01:02,960
And they found a disused stone quarry in a beautiful area.

15
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,157
The price was relatively low,

16
00:01:05,157 --> 00:01:10,430
and they liked the idea of recycling the land, as it were.

17
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:15,843
As it was, the quarry was an ugly blot on the landscape,

18
00:01:15,843 --> 00:01:19,000
and it wasn't productive any longer, either.

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:27,129
They consulted various architects and looked at a number of designs before finally deciding

20
00:01:27,129 --> 00:01:28,290
on one.

21
00:01:28,290 --> 00:01:34,200
As I've said, it was a design for a sort of underground house,

22
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:39,200
and it was built into the earth itself, with two storeys.

23
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:44,784
The north, east and west sides were set in the earth,

24
00:01:44,784 --> 00:01:49,860
and only the sloping, south-facing side was exposed to light.

25
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:55,440
That was made of a double layer of very strong glass.

26
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:02,776
There were also photovoltaic tiles fixed to the top and bottom of this sloping

27
00:02:02,776 --> 00:02:03,300
wall.

28
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:08,270
These are tiles that are designed to store energy from the sun.

29
00:02:08,270 --> 00:02:14,337
And the walls had a layer of foam around them too, to increase the

30
00:02:14,337 --> 00:02:14,770
insulation.

31
00:02:14,770 --> 00:02:21,272
Now, what is of interest to us about this project is the features which

32
00:02:21,272 --> 00:02:23,130
make the building energy-efficient.

33
00:02:23,130 --> 00:02:26,277
Sunlight floods in through the glass wall,

34
00:02:26,277 --> 00:02:32,570
and to maximise it there are lots of mirrors and windows inside the house.

35
00:02:32,570 --> 00:02:35,528
That helps to spread the light around.

36
00:02:35,528 --> 00:02:40,600
So that's the first thing - light is utilised as fully as possible.

37
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:46,990
In addition, the special tiles on the outside convert energy from the sun

38
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:50,430
and generate some of the house's electricity.

39
00:02:50,430 --> 00:02:51,330
In fact,

40
00:02:51,330 --> 00:02:57,630
and it's possible that in future the house may even generate an electricity surplus,

41
00:02:57,630 --> 00:03:03,930
and that the owners will be able to sell some to the national grid.

42
00:03:03,930 --> 00:03:10,330
As well as that, wherever possible, recycled materials have been used.

43
00:03:10,330 --> 00:03:15,570
For example, the floors are made of reclaimed wood.

44
00:03:15,570 --> 00:03:22,117
And the owners haven't bought a single item of new furniture-they just kept what

45
00:03:22,117 --> 00:03:23,520
they already had.

46
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:28,800
And then there's the system for dealing with the waste produced in the house.

47
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:34,808
This is dealt with organically - it's purified by being filtered through reed beds

48
00:03:34,808 --> 00:03:39,430
which have been planted for that purpose in the garden.

49
00:03:39,430 --> 00:03:44,023
So the occupants of the house won't pollute the land

50
00:03:44,023 --> 00:03:46,320
or use any damaging chemicals.

51
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:53,730
It's true that the actual construction of the house was harmful to the environment.

52
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:59,710
Mainly because they had to use massive amounts of concrete - one of the

53
00:03:59,710 --> 00:04:02,930
biggest sources of carbon dioxide in manufacturing.

54
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:08,770
And, as you know, this is very damaging to the environment.

55
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:16,631
In total, the house construction has released 70 tons of carbon dioxide into the

56
00:04:16,631 --> 00:04:17,192
air.

57
00:04:17,192 --> 00:04:20,000
Now that's a frightening thought.

58
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:26,356
However, once the initial 'debt' has been cleared - and it's been calculated that

59
00:04:26,356 --> 00:04:29,290
this will only take fifteen years -

60
00:04:29,290 --> 00:04:34,180
this underground house won't cost anything - environmentally I mean -

61
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:41,787
because unlike ordinary houses, it is run in a way that is completely environmentally

62
00:04:41,787 --> 00:04:42,330
friendly.

63
00:04:42,330 --> 00:04:47,760
So, eco-housing like this is likely to become much more…
